<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Package
  xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/foundation/windows10"
  xmlns:uap="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/uap/windows10"
  xmlns:uap3="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/uap/windows10/3"
  xmlns:com="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/com/windows10"
  xmlns:desktop="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/desktop/windows10"
  xmlns:desktop6="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/desktop/windows10/6"
  xmlns:rescap="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/foundation/windows10/restrictedcapabilities">

  <Identity
    Name="{{PACKAGE_NAME}}"
    Publisher="{{PUBLISHER}}"
    Version="{{VERSION}}"
    ProcessorArchitecture="{{ARCH}}" />

  <Properties>
    <DisplayName>{{DISPLAY_NAME}}</DisplayName>
    <PublisherDisplayName>{{PUBLISHER_DISPLAY_NAME}}</PublisherDisplayName>
    <Logo>Assets\StoreLogo.png</Logo>
    <!--
      What makes `muteWindowsBanners` work inside the package.

      By default a packaged app's HKCU writes are redirected into a private hive
      (`SystemAppData\Helium\User.dat`), so `notifications.rs` writing
      `ShowBanner = 0` lands somewhere only Crest can read and the shell keeps
      drawing its banner from the value it still holds. Measured on 0.7.1: all 46
      entries went to the private hive, the real `…\Notifications\Settings` had
      none. Nothing errors, which is what makes it dangerous — the sweep reports
      success and the feature is silently dead.

      This is the documented opt-out, and it is why `unvirtualizedResources` is in
      the capability list below: the two only work as a pair, and neither does
      anything alone. File-system virtualization is deliberately left ON — writes
      to `%APPDATA%` already reach the real location (verified: the packaged build
      shares `notes.json` and `settings.json` with the NSIS one), so there is
      nothing to buy and turning it off would widen what has to be justified.

      Requires MinVersion 10.0.19041.0, which is why the npm script passes a
      min-windows flag. Crest is a Windows 11 app; the bundler's 17763 default was
      never reachable for it.
    -->
    <desktop6:RegistryWriteVirtualization>disabled</desktop6:RegistryWriteVirtualization>
  </Properties>

  <Dependencies>
    <TargetDeviceFamily Name="Windows.Desktop"
      MinVersion="{{MIN_VERSION}}"
      MaxVersionTested="10.0.22621.0" />
  </Dependencies>

  <Resources>
    <Resource Language="en-us" />
  </Resources>

  <Applications>
    <Application Id="App"
      Executable="{{EXECUTABLE}}"
      EntryPoint="Windows.FullTrustApplication">
      <uap:VisualElements
        DisplayName="{{DISPLAY_NAME}}"
        Description="{{DESCRIPTION}}"
        BackgroundColor="transparent"
        Square150x150Logo="Assets\Square150x150Logo.png"
        Square44x44Logo="Assets\Square44x44Logo.png">
        <uap:DefaultTile Wide310x150Logo="Assets\Wide310x150Logo.png" />
      </uap:VisualElements>

{{EXTENSIONS}}
    </Application>
  </Applications>

  <Capabilities>
    <!--
      Declared here rather than in bundle.config.json because the bundler only
      emits <Capability>, <DeviceCapability> and <rescap:Capability>, and this one
      is a uap3 capability. It is what `notifications.rs` needs: without it,
      UserNotificationListener::RequestAccessAsync returns Denied and the notch's
      notification card and banner are permanently empty.

      General-use, not restricted — it prompts the user for consent at runtime and
      needs no Store approval.

      **Above the generated capability block, not below it, and that is
      load-bearing.** The schema models Capabilities as a sequence: any number of
      Capability / foreign-namespace capabilities, and *then* DeviceCapability.
      The generated block ends with the DeviceCapability for bluetooth, so this
      line after it fails packaging outright with "Element uap3:Capability is
      unexpected … Expecting: DeviceCapability" (MakeAppx C00CE014).

      The same ordering rule is why `restricted` in bundle.config.json cannot be
      combined with `device`: the generator emits restricted capabilities last,
      i.e. after the DeviceCapability, which is invalid for the same reason. If a
      restricted capability is ever genuinely needed, hand-write it here instead.
    -->
    <uap3:Capability Name="userNotificationListener" />
    <!--
      The other half of RegistryWriteVirtualization above; neither works alone.

      **Restricted, so the Store grants it case by case and the submission has to
      justify it.** The justification is the one the app is for: Crest replaces
      Windows' notification banner with its own, and the per-app `ShowBanner`
      value is the only mechanism Windows exposes for suppressing the one it
      draws. Without this the notch shows a notification *next to* the banner it
      was meant to replace, which is the feature inverted rather than merely
      missing.

      Hand-written here rather than in bundle.config.json's `restricted` array on
      purpose: the generator emits restricted capabilities *after* the
      DeviceCapability for bluetooth, which the schema rejects — the same ordering
      rule the uap3 capability above is subject to. See the note there.
    -->
    <rescap:Capability Name="unvirtualizedResources" />
{{CAPABILITIES}}
  </Capabilities>
</Package>
